{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>And the priests and the people, etc.</b> The order of events at the giving of the Torah was as follows. First, He commanded Moses, ״והגבלת את העם״ — that he set a fixed boundary for the people on the mountain that they should not cross it; and this is what Moses said to Him: ״כי אתה העדותה בנו לאמר הגבל את ההר וקדשתו״ — for the boundary of the people is the boundary of the mountain. After that, He warned them to separate for three days from all impurity and required them to immerse, as it is written, ״ויקדש את העם ויכבסו שמלותם״. And on the third day in the morning — which is the sixth of Sivan — there were sounds and lightning and a heavy cloud upon the mountain and a very loud shofar blast, and still the Shekhinah had not descended, in the manner of what is written (I Kings 19:11): ״ורוח גדולה וחזק מפרק הרים ומשבר סלעים לפני ה׳ לא ברוח ה׳״. Then they were gripped by terror from the dread of those sounds, as it says, ״ויחרד כל העם״, etc., and Moses strengthened their hearts to bring them out to meet Elohim, ״ויתיצבו בתחתית ההר״. And while they were there at the foot of the mountain, waiting, the Blessed One descended upon the mountain in fire, ״ויעל עשנו עד לב השמים חשך ענן וערפל״, and the mountain itself trembled and shuddered as happens in the earthquake called זלזל״ה, or even more so. And so said David, peace be upon him (Psalms 68:9): ״ארץ רעשה אף שמים נטפו מפני אלהים זה סיני מפני אלהים אלהי ישראל״ — the meaning of נטפו is dropped fire, and the meaning of זה סיני is burned Sinai, from the root (Daniel 3:22) ״ואתונא איזה יתירא״, and likewise למיזא לאתונא. And so we find with Deborah the prophetess (Judges 5:4): ״ה׳ בצאתך משעיר בצעדך משדה אדום ארץ רעשה גם שמים נטפו גם עבים נטפו מים״. And so Scripture says (Psalms 114:4): ״ההרים רקדו כאילים״, etc. And it is not a metaphor, just as ״הים ראה וינוס״, etc., is not a metaphor. And so said the prophet (Jeremiah 46:18): ״כי כתבור בהרים וככרמל בים יבא״, and our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted that at the time of the giving of the Torah these mountains were planted in the land of Israel, and Carmel crossed over the sea. After that, the shofar sound grew very strong, and then the people saw and trembled and stood farther off from the boundary." "<b>And you should know</b> that the passage ״וכל העם רואים את הקולות״, etc., and ״וירא העם וינועו״ — this was before the giving of the Torah, for then they said to Moses, ״דבר אתה עמנו ונשמעה״, because at the sight and the hearing of those sounds their bearings were turned upside down and they could not endure, and if they were to hear the speech from the mouth of the Almighty they would die. And Moses strengthened their hearts and said to them, ״אל תיראו כי לבעבור נסות אתכם״, etc. And then they listened to him, ״ויעמוד העם מרחוק״ — for they were not appeased nor did they consent to all his words to draw near to the boundary. And Moses drew near to the fog — he did not enter within it — and then Elohim spoke the Ten Commandments." "<b>And the priests and the people shall not break through to ascend to HaShem.</b> But was it not already mentioned above, ״רד העד בעם פן יהרסו אל ה׳ לראות״? Rather, it appears that there Moses understood that the warning he was to give them was that they should not ascend the mountain and should not approach the marked boundary to see with the sense of sight, and Moses replied to this, ״לא יוכל העם לעלות אל הר סיני כי אתה העדותה בנו״ — in the language of warning, as it is written: ״והגבלת את העם סביב לאמר השמרו לכם עלות בהר ונגוע בקצהו כל הנוגע בהר מות יומת לא תגע בו יד״. And this is the import of the added letters of the word העדותה. Therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, needed to return and explain to him that the warning is not about approaching the boundary, for they have already been permitted and warned concerning that; rather, it is about the nearness of apprehension and the gazing of thought. And this is what He said to him: ״לך רד ועלית אתה ואהרן עמך והכהנים והעם אל יהרסו לעלות״ — He used the term לעלות, meaning an ascent of thought higher than the capacity of apprehension. Therefore He equated them all as one — the priests and the people, ״והיה כעם ככהן״ — that they should not gaze at nor desire to apprehend what is not apprehensible." "<b>And you should examine</b> the verse ״פן יהרסו אל ה׳ לראות״ — why did it not say ״פן יהרסו לראות את ה׳״, just as it says here, ״אל יהרסו לעלות אל ה׳״? And it is possible to say that had it said so, it would appear from this that the seeing is possible, only that He prevents it. Therefore He deferred the seeing and said, ״פן יהרסו אל ה׳ לראות״ — lest their thought break through, that they would think to see Him — and through this He explained that seeing is not merely impossible but rather a thing that is precluded, in the manner of what is written (Exodus 33): ״כי לא יראני האדם וחי״."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :register {:flags {1 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:01:33.781129010Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}